{"unit_id": "Neferuni", "choices": ["C4"], "reasoning": "Record 1 divides most naturally as Neferu + ni, the element Neferu being Nfr.w as in the listed Neferu, Neferuptah and Neferure. C4 Nfr.w-Nj.t (Neferu-Neith): nfr.w gives Neferu, and Nj.t gives 'ni' once the feminine t is dropped, which the rules allow. That gives Neferu-Ni(t), and Nit is a recognised rendering of Neith. Dedetanuq (Dd.t-Ꜥnq.t), where the goddess Anuket appears as 'anuq' without its t, supports this reading. Accepted, though with some uncertainty, since Dodson & Hilton might be expected to print 'Neith' in full. C1 Nfr-wn.t (Nefer-wenet): the consonants could be forced (Nefer-un, t dropped), but the final 'i' would then stand for no sign. Record 1 also reads as Neferu-ni rather than Nefer-wen(e)t, so it does not render the word wenet. Rejected. C2 Nfr-nw-j (Nefer-nu-i): the words are nfr + nw + j, with n before w. Record 1 has u before n ('-uni'), so it renders wn rather than nw. That only fits if the u is treated as an inserted vowel and the w is dropped, which does not render 'Nefer-nu-i'. Rejected. C3 Nfr-jwn.w (Nefer-iunu): the words are nfr + Iunu (Heliopolis). Record 1's '-uni' does not render 'iunu', since the final w would have to appear as 'i' and the word would not be recognisable. Rejected."}
```

